  -> §9 expects ~120-170 shots in a 10-minute video at typical editing pace.
     Scaled from this corpus: 40 shots per 10 minutes.
     These short loops do NOT look like §9's expectation, and that is a PROPERTY OF
     THIS CORPUS, not a refutation of §9: a 7-second hero loop and a screen-recorded
     slide deck are not edited at broadcast pace. Nothing about a 10-minute film is
     claimed from these numbers.

     NOTE the ordering surfaced above: on THIS corpus the I-frame count and the shot
     count are not the same quantity and need not be ordered the way §9's example
     implies. An encoder emits an I-frame on a fixed GOP cadence whether or not the
     picture changed, so on low-cut footage there are MORE I-frames than shots — the
     I-frame is a cheap CANDIDATE SET, and shot detection is what turns it into a
     semantic unit. That is exactly why the pipeline has both stages.

