=== 7. NEAR-DUPLICATE VIDEO (p63 acceptance 4) =========================
  A SECOND PASS over the manifest section 6 just wrote, in the shape of Meta's vPDQ:
  hash every extracted keyframe, keep the hashes as a per-video SEQUENCE in shot
  order, and compare two videos by how much of one sequence is found in the other.
  Nothing in index-image changed to make this possible — Hash64::dhash and
  Hash256::pdq were already there, and a shot hash is the same hash column an image
  document already carries.

  BOTH code lengths are computed, because a Hamming threshold is meaningless without
  the code length beside it: 31 bits is 12 % of a PDQ code and 48 % of a 64-bit one.
    dHash  64-bit,  crate default radius HASH64_NEAR_MAX  = 4  (6.2 % of the code)
    PDQ   256-bit,  crate default radius HASH256_NEAR_MAX = 31 (12.1 % of the code)

  ORDER-INSENSITIVE, and that is the honest limit. Similarity(A,B) is the fraction of
  A's shots that find a match ANYWHERE in B — B is searched as a BAG of shot hashes,
  not as a sequence. So this detects a re-upload, a re-encode, a resize and a trimmed
  cut, and it does NOT detect a re-ordered edit as anything other than a duplicate.
  A true vPDQ that scored the ORDER as well would need an alignment step this does
  not have; what is built here is a bag-of-shot-hashes and is reported as one.

  --- the hash pass ---
    265 keyframe hashed, 0 failed, 3.63 s (13.68 ms per keyframe,
    JPEG decode INCLUDED — the decode is the host's cost, both hashes together are a
    small fraction of it)

    0 keyframe(s) produced an ALL-ZERO PDQ code. Hash256::pdq returns zero
    for a frame with no two-dimensional structure — a solid colour, a fade to black.
    Two such frames sit at distance 0 BY CONSTRUCTION, so they match each other and
    everything else that is flat. That is a property of the code, not a duplicate, and
    it is stated here rather than left to inflate a similarity quietly.

