=== WHAT THIS RUN SURFACED =============================================
  1. VIDEO NEEDED NO NEW INDEX TYPE, AND THAT IS THE HEADLINE.
     Everything this benchmark built is HOST-SIDE: decode, shot-detect, pick the
     middle frame, emit a manifest. Not one line of `index-image` or `index-text`
     changed, and none needed to. A shot is an ImageDoc with a (video, start_ms,
     end_ms) and an embedding; the fusion layer, the vector column and the hash
     column already index it. p63 names that asymmetry as the strongest available
     evidence that the p59 architecture is right, and this run is that evidence.

  2. THE CHEAP DECODE DOES NOT PAY FOR THE SHOT DETECTION.
     `-skip_frame nokey` is 1.87x faster than a full decode here, but the scene
     scorer needs every frame and costs 0.73 s per video-minute — more than the full
     decode it rides on. The keyframe speedup is real and it is NOT the pipeline's
     total cost. A production build would run detection once at ingest and keep the
     shot list, which is precisely what the manifest is for.

  3. THE CORPUS IS THE LIMIT, NOT THE CODE.
     27 unique video, 65.72 video-minutes, no relevance judgements. Every count above
     is exact and reproducible; every claim about 10-minute broadcast-paced footage
     belongs to §9 and is cited, not re-derived. p63's acceptance 3 stays open until
     a labelled clip set exists on this machine.

  4. NEAR-DUPLICATE VIDEO IS A SECOND PASS, AND IT NEEDED NO NEW ENGINE CODE EITHER.
     Section 7 hashes the 265 extracted keyframe(s) with Hash64::dhash and
     Hash256::pdq — both already in index-image — and compares two videos by how much
     of one shot-hash sequence is found in the other. The manifest was the whole seam.
     The limit is stated there rather than hidden: the comparison is ORDER-INSENSITIVE,
     which is the honest ceiling of a bag of shot hashes, and this corpus was already
     deduplicated by sha256, so an exact duplicate cannot appear in it by construction.

